2. **Manage State Consistency:**
   Ensure that all relevant data (like blog posts) are appropriately initialized or present before running tests or interacting with the application. This includes verifying that your database or text files contain the required initial data.

3. **Error Handling:**
   Implement proper error handling and checks when loading data. For example, handle cases where a user tries to access a post that doesn't exist in a user-friendly manner, rather than relying on the application to crash or throw unhandled errors.

4. **Decouple Data Logic from Business Logic:**
   Ensure separation of concerns by structuring your code so that data access components are separate from business logic. This makes debugging easier and improves maintainability, especially when integrating with a front-end or a UI framework.

5. **Review and Test UI Interactions:**
   Regularly review the UI code and its interaction with backend functionalities. Make sure that any additions to the UI (like new post links or buttons) are supported by backend logic, ensuring that users will not encounter broken links or empty states.

By focusing on these areas, you can build a more stable and maintainable codebase that reduces the chances of failures during testing and improves the overall user experience.|
